 ## Overview
-RePaint-NeRF is a framework for editing 3D content in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) using text prompts by leveraging a pre-trained diffusion model to guide changes in designated 3D content, ensuring view consistency and scene integrity [S1]. It solves the problem of inconsistent edits that arise when applying 2D image editing models (like InstructPix2Pix) independently to rendered frames [S2]. Unlike [Instruct-NeRF2NeRF Optimization](15089-instruct-nerf2nerf-optimization.md), which relies on a single alternating update scheme without explicit semantic masking for the target object, RePaint-NeRF explicitly extracts a view-consistent semantic mask in a first stage to isolate the target region, then uses a second stage to guide the diffusion model and NeRF optimization specifically within that mask while preserving the background [S1].
+RePaint-NeRF is a framework for editing 3D content in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) using text prompts by leveraging a pre-trained diffusion model to guide changes in designated 3D content, ensuring view consistency and scene integrity [S1]. It operates by iteratively alternating between two phases: (1) editing the 'dataset' of NeRF input images using an image-conditioned diffusion model, and (2) updating the underlying 3D representation (NeRF) to incorporate the edited images through standard NeRF training [S2]. This iterative process allows for the gradual percolation of diffusion priors into the 3D scene, resolving inconsistencies that arise from applying 2D edits independently to each view [S2]. It builds on [Diffusion-guided editing](15110-diffusion-guided-editing.md) by using an iterative dataset update scheme rather than direct gradient-based guidance (like SDS) for the NeRF parameters [S2].
 
 ## How it works
-RePaint-NeRF operates in two distinct stages: Semantic Mask Extraction and Text-to-3D Content Editing. It takes a pre-captured NeRF scene, its source data (images, camera poses, calibration), and a natural-language instruction as input, and produces an edited NeRF and edited input images [S2].
+RePaint-NeRF consists of two distinct stages: Semantic Mask Extraction and Text-to-3D Content Editing. The method takes a pre-captured NeRF scene, its source data (images, camera poses, calibration), and a natural-language instruction as input, and produces an edited NeRF and edited input images [S2].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
 *   **Inputs:**
-    *   A reconstructed NeRF scene (typically Instant-NGP [S1]).
-    *   A set of captured images $I^0_v$ and corresponding camera poses/calibration (from COLMAP) [S1, S2].
-    *   A natural-language editing instruction (e.g., "turn him into Albert Einstein") [S2].
-    *   A background prompt (BGT) to guide the filling of exposed areas [S1].
+    *   Pre-trained NeRF model $f_\theta$ (typically Instant-NGP [S1] or Nerfacto [S2]).
+    *   Training dataset: Set of images $I^0_v$, camera poses, and calibration parameters (typically from COLMAP) [S1, S2].
+    *   Text instruction $c_T$ (e.g., "turn him into Albert Einstein") [S2].
+    *   Optional: Background prompt $c_{BGT}$ (e.g., "leaves" if editing a rose) [S1].
 *   **Outputs:**
-    *   An edited NeRF model.
-    *   Edited versions of the input dataset images [S2].
+    *   Edited NeRF parameters $\theta$.
+    *   Edited dataset images $I^i_v$.
 
 ### Data Structures
-*   **NeRF Model:** Instant-NGP [Müller et al., 2022], based on a multi-resolution hash grid structure [S1].
-*   **Feature Map:** Size $H \times W \times 512$. Interpolated from LSeg [Li et al., 2022] [S1].
-*   **Depth Map:** Size $H \times W \times 1$. Normalized to range 0 to 1. Predicted by MiDAS [Ranftl et al., 2022] [S1].
-*   **Semantic Mask:** $I_{mask}^{H \times W \times 1}$. Binary mask indicating the target object [S1].
+*   **NeRF Model:** Instant-NGP [Müller et al., 2022] based on multi-resolution hash grid structure [S1]. Stores color $C$ and density $\sigma$ [Parent].
+*   **Semantic Feature Field:** A view-independent feature field $s: \mathbf{x} \to \mathbf{f}$, where $\mathbf{f}$ is a high-dimensional vector with semantic information [S1].
